---
/**
 * /contact — HOW TO REACH DIRECTHIRED, and where it is.
 *
 * Core-pages design spec §3.2 gives it five parts and no others: details
 * from company.ts · office · hours · socials · EmploymentAgency structured
 * data · CTA. Spec §4 gives it ONE intent — local — which is the only
 * intent no other page on this site owns, and the reason the title and
 * description below are written around a place and a phone number rather
 * than around a promise.
 *
 * IT IS LINKED FROM THE FOOTER, NOT THE NAVIGATION. Spec §3.4 records that
 * as a decision taken by default: the header nav is at its width budget
 * (tests/header-fit.test.ts pins the item count that was measured), and the
 * footer already carries the same details as text. So src/lib/nav.ts gains
 * `footerItems` rather than an eighth `navItems` entry — adding one to
 * navItems fails header-fit, correctly, and would be the wrong fix for it.
 *
 * NOT ONE VALUE ON THIS PAGE IS TYPED. The phone number, the display
 * spelling of it, the email, the address, the opening hours, both social
 * profiles, the licence and the WhatsApp deep link all resolve from
 * src/data/company.ts, and the address is assembled exactly the way
 * Footer.astro and /about assemble it. That is not tidiness: master brief
 * §51 asks for CONSISTENT company details as the Singapore-presence signal,
 * and a contact page is the one page where a second copy of a detail is
 * both most likely and most damaging. tests/company.test.ts sweeps every
 * surface for a retyped licence; nothing sweeps for a retyped phone number,
 * which is exactly why this file must not start one.
 *
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE.
 *
 *   - NO DESCRIPTION OF THE OFFICE, no district characterisation, no
 *     "drop in", no team size, no photograph. /about had to delete "and so
 *     is the office those placements are handled from" for inferring one
 *     office and what happens in it from an address string; the same
 *     sentence is even easier to write on this page and is equally
 *     unsupplied. What ships is the address, as a fact.
 *   - NO RESPONSE PROMISE BEYOND THE PUBLISHED ONE. "A consultant reviews
 *     every submission and aims to respond within 1 business day" is
 *     src/content/faq/submit-requirements.md verbatim — client-approved
 *     copy, already on /faq and the homepage. "We reply fast", "we are
 *     always available", "call any time" are not, and the 24-hour opening
 *     figure is not licence to invent one.
 *   - NO REGISTERED ENTITY AND NO UEN. src/data/company.ts's own docblock
 *     scopes both to /about — "they appear nowhere else on the site" — and
 *     that is a deliberate division: /about publishes the company's
 *     particulars as a register entry, this page publishes how to reach it.
 *     The licence number is the one credential that belongs on both,
 *     because a family checking a Singapore agency checks the licence, and
 *     it points at /about for the rest rather than restating it.
 *   - NO CONTACT FORM. Spec §2.6.5: the employer requirement form stays on
 *     the legacy site, so the primary conversion path leaves this domain.
 *     A second, different form here would be a new form nobody asked for.
 *
 * SECTION GROUNDS run cream / white / teal / cream across the four blocks
 * (.contact-start, .contact-direct, .contact-office, FinalCta), registered
 * and asserted in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts. The brand wash lands on the
 * office block for the same reason it lands on /about's Singapore block:
 * --color-surface-teal exists to give #00a4a6 real graphic area, and the
 * block about where the company physically IS is the one place on a page
 * where a brand ground carries meaning rather than decoration. No second
 * dark band — spec §6 keeps block 07's --color-deep as the site's only
 * palette register shift, and §3.2 does not list TwoSidedMatch here.
 *
 * THE CTA PAIR SITS IN THE HERO AS WELL AS IN FinalCta, and /about
 * deliberately does the opposite. That page's argument was that four short
 * blocks put a second pair of buttons a few hundred pixels above the
 * closing one, i.e. two conversion surfaces in one screen. It does not
 * reach this page: the pair here is on the FIRST screen and FinalCta is
 * below three blocks of details, which is the /pricing arrangement rather
 * than the /about one. A contact page whose primary action is three blocks
 * down is a contact page that hid the thing it exists for.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import Button from '../components/Button.astro'
import FinalCta from '../sections/FinalCta.astro'
import { company } from '../data/company'
import { defaultWhatsappUrl } from '../lib/whatsapp'
import { employmentAgencySchema } from '../lib/structured-data'

const whatsappHref = defaultWhatsappUrl()

/*
 * Assembled from company.ts exactly as Footer.astro and /about assemble it.
 * Never retyped — see this file's header. The postal code is part of the
 * string rather than a separate row because that is how a Singapore address
 * is written and how a visitor will paste it into a map.
 */
const addressLine = `${company.address.street}, ${company.address.locality} ${company.address.postalCode}`

/*
 * Spec §4: this page owns LOCAL intent, which is the one intent no other
 * page claims. So the title carries the two things a local search resolves
 * on — what the business is and where it is — rather than the brand-and-
 * trust framing /about and /why-directhired share. The licence resolves
 * from company.ts like every other value here; a literal fails
 * tests/company.test.ts's never-retyped sweep, which now covers src/lib and
 * src/data as well as the markup.
 */
const title = 'Contact DirectHired — Phone, WhatsApp and Office in Singapore'
const description =
  `Contact DirectHired, a licensed Singapore maid agency: call ${company.phoneDisplay}, ` +
  `email ${company.email}, or find the office address and opening hours.`
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  {/*
    EmploymentAgency structured data — spec §4's local-intent signal for
    this page, and the reason §3.2 names it here by name.

    THE SAME FUNCTION THE HOMEPAGE CALLS, not a second schema. Every value
    in it resolves from src/data/company.ts (name, telephone, email, the
    postal address, both social profiles), so the two pages cannot describe
    the business differently — which is the entire point of a LocalBusiness
    subtype: a search engine reconciling two pages of one site must find one
    business, one address and one number. Adding a literal to that function
    scored a fully green suite once; it does not now, and
    tests/company.test.ts records the mutation that proved it.
  */}
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(employmentAgencySchema())} slot="head" />

  <section class="contact-start">
    <Container>
      <h1>Talk to DirectHired.</h1>

      {/*
        The published response line, verbatim from
        src/content/faq/submit-requirements.md — client-approved copy that
        already renders on /faq and the homepage. It is the only response
        figure anybody has supplied, and it is a TARGET ("aims to"), which
        is how it was given and how it must stay. Do not upgrade it to a
        guarantee and do not add a second, faster one for WhatsApp.
      */}
      <p class="contact-lede">
        Send us your requirements and a consultant reviews every submission, aiming to respond
        within 1 business day. If you would rather ask something first, the details below reach us
        directly.
      </p>

      <div class="contact-cta">
        <Button variant="primary" href={company.requirementFormUrl}>Submit Your Requirements</Button>
        <Button variant="secondary" href={whatsappHref} external>WhatsApp Us</Button>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section class="contact-direct">
    <Container>
      <h2 class="section-title">Ways to reach us</h2>

      {/*
        A row each, term in a fixed column and the value beside it — the
        site's established fact-table shape (/about's record block,
        /find-your-helper's timings). A card each would box three short
        facts into three surfaces on a white ground, which is the generic-
        UI-kit reading Card.astro's own header warns about.

        Every href is built, never typed: tel: from phoneE164 (E.164 is the
        form a dialler wants), mailto: from email, and the WhatsApp link
        from defaultWhatsappUrl() — the same single-sourced deep link every
        secondary CTA on the site uses, carrying the same pre-filled
        message. tests/links.test.ts and tests/whatsapp.test.ts both hold
        that.
      */}
      <dl class="channels">
        <div class="channel-row">
          <dt>Phone</dt>
          <dd class="channel-value">
            <a href={`tel:${company.phoneE164}`}>{company.phoneDisplay}</a>
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="channel-row">
          <dt>WhatsApp</dt>
          <dd class="channel-value">
            <a href={whatsappHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              {company.phoneDisplay}
            </a>
          </dd>
          <dd class="channel-note">Opens a message to the same number, ready to send.</dd>
        </div>

        <div class="channel-row">
          <dt>Email</dt>
          <dd class="channel-value">
            <a href={`mailto:${company.email}`}>{company.email}</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </Container>
  </section>

  <section class="contact-office">
    <Container>
      <h2 class="section-title">Where we are</h2>

      <div class="office">
        <div class="office-address">
          <p class="office-label">Office</p>
          {/*
            The address as a fact, at display size, with tabular figures so
            the postal code reads as a number — the treatment /about gives
            the same string. Nothing describes the building, the district or
            what happens inside it: none of that is supplied, and all of it
            is the natural next sentence.
          */}
          <p class="address">{addressLine}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="office-meta">
          <p class="office-label">Opening hours</p>
          <p class="hours">{company.openingHours}</p>

          <p class="office-label office-label-spaced">Follow DirectHired</p>
          <ul class="socials">
            <li>
              <a href={company.socials.facebook} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Facebook</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={company.socials.instagram} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                Instagram
              </a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/*
        The licence, and only the licence. It is the one credential a family
        checking a Singapore agency actually checks, and a contact page that
        made them go and look for it would be withholding the useful half of
        "who am I calling". The registered entity, the UEN and the track
        record stay on /about — company.ts scopes the first two there by
        name — so this points at that page instead of restating it.
      */}
      <p class="licence">
        MOM Employment Agency licence {company.momLicence}. The rest of the company’s particulars
        are on the <a href="/about">about page</a>.
      </p>
    </Container>
  </section>

  {/*
    The page's closing conversion surface, framed for this page. It says
    what the details above cannot: a phone number tells you how to ask, and
    the submission is what actually starts a placement. No response figure
    here — the hero already carries the published one, and repeating it
    twice on one page would read as a promise being pressed rather than
    stated.
  */}
  <FinalCta
    heading="Or start with your requirements."
    lede="Tell us about your household and what you are looking for, and a consultant will pick it up from there."
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .contact-start {
    /*
     * --space-12 top, --space-section bottom — the treatment every other
     * page gives its opening block, for the same reason: this block carries
     * the page's hero, and a full --space-section (128px at ≥1280) above an
     * image-less single-column headline spends the first screen on cream.
     */
    padding-block: var(--space-12) var(--space-section);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .contact-start h1 {
    margin: 0;
    /*
     * 16ch. "Talk to DirectHired." is one 20-character line and sets on a
     * single line at every width from 320 up at --size-h1's clamped scale;
     * the measure is here to stop it stretching if the headline is ever
     * lengthened, not to break this one. /about's 20ch is a MEASURED value
     * for a 45-character sentence and does not transfer.
     */
    max-width: 16ch;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h1);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .contact-lede {
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-lede);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .contact-cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .contact-direct {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* White, between the cream hero above and the teal wash below.
       Registered in tests/section-rhythm.test.ts; the grounds have to be
       judged as a sequence, so do not flip this one alone. */
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  /* Shared by both page-local <h2>s, at --size-h3 rather than --size-h2:
     these head a sub-part of a page the <h1> has already opened, and
     setting them at section-header scale would put three competing display
     sizes on one screen. Same size and role as /about's .section-title. */
  .section-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .channels {
    max-width: 60rem;
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 0;
  }

  /* The rule between rows rather than around them — /about's record block
     and /find-your-helper's timings use the same shape. --color-border is
     a decorative hairline (~1.3:1, see tokens.css); it carries no text and
     bounds no control, and it is never --color-brand-teal, which is
     reserved as a drawn line for block 07's crossbar and FinalCta's mark. */
  .channel-row {
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .channel-row:last-child {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  /* The site's established eyebrow treatment (TrustBadge's .trust-label,
     /about's .record dt). --tracking-wide's contract requires
     text-transform: uppercase in the same rule — tests/tokens.test.ts
     asserts it, and it scans src/pages. */
  .channels dt {
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .channel-value {
    max-width: 40ch;
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* --color-accent (6.03:1 on cream, 5.83:1 on white, 5.54:1 on the teal
     wash), never --color-brand-teal, which measures 2.89:1 and carries no
     text anywhere on this site. */
  .channel-value a,
  .licence a,
  .socials a {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  .channel-value a:hover,
  .licence a:hover,
  .socials a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    border-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  .channel-note {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .contact-office {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /*
     * The page's one section in the brand wash. --color-surface-teal is 8%
     * --color-brand-teal over cream (tokens.css) and exists to give the
     * logo colour real graphic AREA without ever setting text in it;
     * measured, --color-ink reads 12.87:1 on it, --color-ink-muted 7.32:1
     * and --color-accent 5.54:1. One per page is the rule —
     * tests/section-rhythm.test.ts asserts it.
     */
    background: var(--color-surface-teal);
  }

  .office {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* The site's established eyebrow treatment. Same contract as .channels dt
     above: --tracking-wide only on uppercase. */
  .office-label {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-small);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  .office-label-spaced {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  /*
   * The address and the hours at display size — both are facts rather than
   * prose, and the address is the one thing on this page a visitor is most
   * likely to copy. Tabular figures for the same reason TrustBadge uses
   * them: a postal code should read as a number.
   */
  .address,
  .hours {
    max-width: 30ch;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-h3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .socials {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
  }

  .licence {
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: var(--space-12) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    /* Full ink rather than --color-ink-muted on this ground: the wash lifts
       the page's lightness, and the muted step that reads as secondary on
       white starts to read as faded here. Same finding as /about's. */
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* 48em = --bp-tablet (tokens.css); the literal is repeated by hand
     because custom properties do not resolve inside a media condition. */
  @media (min-width: 48em) {
    /*
     * The term column is fixed so all three values start on one axis;
     * `minmax(0, 1fr)` on the second track lets it shrink below its
     * content's min-content width instead of pushing the row wider than the
     * container — which a long email address on a narrow tablet will do.
     *
     * The dt is pinned to the first cell and every dd to the second, in
     * explicit placement rather than auto-flow: the WhatsApp row has a
     * value AND a note, and auto-flow places the note back under the term
     * in column 1, filling the term column with body copy.
     */
    .channel-row {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 12rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: var(--space-8);
      align-items: baseline;
    }

    .channels dt {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    .channels dd {
      grid-column: 2;
    }

    .channel-value {
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    /* Address left, hours and socials right. Same geometry and the same
       reason as /about's .presence: single-column, the one section that
       spends the brand ground would run tall with its content in the left
       60% and nothing beside it. */
    .office {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 20rem;
      column-gap: var(--space-12);
      align-items: start;
    }
  }
</style>
